# netsurf: one fetch and one evaluation per module URL, across
# parallel loads
#
# Roadmap WS-C, duplicate-fetch measurement (the task left in flight at
# the 05:18 session-limit banner): with the 159/160 pipelines running in
# parallel, the same module URL was being fetched -- and evaluated --
# once per load that wanted it (a module in a static graph AND
# import()ed: 2 fetches; the same import() twice: 2; the same <script
# type=module src> twice: 2; a module script AND import() of it: 2).
#
# - macsurf_qjs.c: the module card (module_entry) records the page
#   fetch: js_module_page_fetch() marks the URL the page is fetching
#   itself (a <script type=module src>) and clears it on completion;
#   non-blocking loads (import() graphs) WAIT for the page
#   fetch instead of fetching the URL a second time, and a blocking
#   crawl finds the card registered and uses it. js_module_known()
#   says the registry already has (or is loading) the URL.
#   g_module_refetches counts the fetches that found their URL
#   registered by the time they got to it; the count is logged per
#   fetch and in every graph summary line (the visible count).
#   Compiled bytes are cached on the card (the card entry is now
#   written for external modules only: an inline module
#   name is not stable across visits). The registry stays the
#   single source of truth -- two loads of the same URL get the same
#   module namespace, as before.
# - script.c: a <script type=module src> whose URL is already known
#   (import()ed earlier, the same src twice) is not fetched again:
#   it queues as an inline placeholder and runs from the registry in
#   its turn among the deferred scripts (HTML module-map semantics:
#   the URL, not the element, identifies the module). A module script
#   the page itself fetches registers its page fetch first, so the
#   parallel import()s of the same URL wait for it.
# Test: JS-module-dedupe.html 5/5, gated in run-vita-sim.sh (httpd
# /fetch-count reports GET counts per path, so the page asserts the
# server saw each of its 9 module files fetched exactly once, with
# both shared leaves served after 1 s so every load is in flight at
# once). Full gate exit 0.
